      "Language": "pol",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "5426 males aged 40-59 of 14 Warsaw factories underwent the study to evaluate the relationship between coronary artery disease risk factors and education levels. Greater mean body mass, total cholesterol level and greater mean value of Rose's coefficient were significantly more frequently observed in men with secondary and university education. Persons with technical or elementary education have been significantly more frequently smoking cigarettes and overweighted."},
